## 1. The consumer API

What a dev writes, end to end:

```ts
import { Spectrum } from "spectrum-ts";
import { imessage } from "spectrum-ts/providers/imessage";

const app = await Spectrum({
  providers: [
    imessage.config({
      clients: [{ address, token, phone: "+15550100" }],
      readReceipts: true, // opt in — off by default
    }),
  ],
});

for await (const [space, message] of app.messages) {
  switch (message.content.type) {
    case "text":
      await message.reply(`echo: ${message.content.text}`);
      break;

    case "read": {
      // `read` is one content type used in both directions (same house rule as
      // `reaction` and the membership types). Inbound = someone read us.
      if (message.direction !== "inbound") {
        break;
      }
      const target = message.content.target; // the message WE sent
      console.log(
        `${message.sender?.id} read "${target.id}" at ${message.timestamp.toISOString()}`
      );
      break;
    }

    default:
      break; // <- the arm that makes this opt-in, not default-on
  }
}
```

The exact types they see:

```ts
// message: Message
message.direction              // "inbound"
message.sender                 // User | undefined  -> the READER. Always set for iMessage (see §5.5)
message.timestamp              // Date              -> event.readAt: when the reader's device marked it read
message.space                  // Space             -> === the `space` from the tuple

message.content                // narrowed to `Read` by `case "read":`
// export interface Read { type: "read"; target: Message }

message.content.target         // Message
message.content.target.id      // string    -> the iMessage guid of the message the agent sent
message.content.target.direction   // "outbound"
message.content.target.content     // Content   -> the text/attachment/etc. we sent
message.content.target.edit(…)     // available: the target is built as outbound
message.content.target.unsend()    // available
```

Narrowed for iMessage extras:

```ts
import { imessage } from "spectrum-ts/providers/imessage";

const im = imessage(message);
im.sender?.address;  // "+15551234567"
im.sender?.service;  // "iMessage" | "SMS" | "RCS" | "unknown"
```

Aggregating "everyone has read it" in a group (the shape consumers will actually need — see §5.4):

```ts
const readers = new Map<string, Set<string>>(); // targetId -> reader ids

case "read": {
  if (message.direction !== "inbound") break;
  const set = readers.get(message.content.target.id) ?? new Set();
  set.add(message.sender?.id ?? "");
  readers.set(message.content.target.id, set);
  const members = await space.getMembers();
  if (set.size >= members.length) {
    console.log(`everyone read ${message.content.target.id}`);
  }
  break;
}
```

**Design invariant to document loudly:** `message.sender` is the reader; `message.content.target` is ours. Never the other way round.

