```ts
export interface IMessageStreamOptions {
  /** Surface inbound `message.read` events as `read` content. Default false. */
  readonly readReceipts?: boolean | undefined;
}

export const messages = (
  clients: RemoteClient[],
  projectConfig?: ProjectData | undefined,
  profileSyncGate?: ProfileSyncGate | undefined,
  options?: IMessageStreamOptions | undefined
): ManagedStream<IMessageMessage> => {
  const pollCache = getPollCache(clients);
  const staticShareEnabled = projectConfig?.profile?.imessageSynced === true;
  const recover = getCloudRecover(clients);
  // Group events are mode-derived (shared lines cannot subscribe); read
  // receipts are config-derived (every mode receives them, but surfacing them
  // is a behavior change). Different gates, deliberately.
  const features: StreamFeatures = {
    includeGroupEvents: !isSharedMode(clients),
    includeReadReceipts: options?.readReceipts === true,
  };
  return mergeStreams(
    clients.map((entry) => {
      const tracker =
        staticShareEnabled || profileSyncGate
          ? getContactShareTracker(entry.client)
          : undefined;
      return clientStream(
        entry.client,
        pollCache,
        entry.phone,
        features,
        tracker ? contactShareHandler(tracker, profileSyncGate) : undefined,
        recover
      );
    })
  );
};
```

Plus the import at the top: `import { readReceiptMessageId, toReadReceiptMessages } from "./read-receipts";`

`streamLabel`'s closed union `"messages" | "polls" | "groups"` (`:50-54`) needs **no change** — read receipts ride the existing `messages` stream, not a fourth one.

### 3.8 `packages/imessage/src/remote/api.ts:58-63` — widen the re-export

```ts
export const messages = (
  clients: RemoteClient[],
  projectConfig: ProjectData | undefined,
  profileSyncGate?: ProfileSyncGate | undefined,
  options?: IMessageStreamOptions | undefined
): ManagedStream<IMessageMessage> =>
  remoteMessages(clients, projectConfig, profileSyncGate, options);
```

with `import { type IMessageStreamOptions, messages as remoteMessages } from "./stream";` at `:51`.

### 3.9 `packages/imessage/src/index.ts:621-622` — pass the config

```ts
  messages: ({ client, config, projectConfig }) =>
    remoteMessages(client, projectConfig, getProfileSyncGate(client), {
      readReceipts: config.readReceipts,
    }),
```

`EventProducer` (`packages/core/src/platform/types.ts:147-160`) already supplies `config: z.infer<_ConfigSchema>` in its ctx, so this needs no core change.

### 3.10 `packages/imessage/src/types.ts:25-27` — the flag

```ts
export const configSchema = z.strictObject({
  clients: clientEntry.or(z.array(clientEntry)).optional(),
  /**
   * Surface **inbound** read receipts on `app.messages`: when a recipient reads
   * a message the agent sent, a `read` Message arrives with
   * `content.target` = the message that was read and `sender` = the reader.
   *
   * Off by default. A group chat emits one event per participant per message
   * read, and enabling it introduces a content type that existing
   * `switch (message.content.type)` loops have never seen — several of which
   * have a `default:` arm that replies to the user.
   *
   * Unrelated to sending read receipts: `space.read(message)` /
   * `message.read()` work regardless of this flag.
   */
  readReceipts: z.boolean().optional(),
});
```

`packages/imessage/src/types.ts` `messageSchema` (`:70-74`) is **unchanged** — see §5.3, we deliberately add no `readAt` extra.

---

## 4. Opt-in mechanism — evaluation and recommendation

| Pattern | Precedent | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| **Mode-derived** (like `includeGroupEvents = !isSharedMode(clients)`, `stream.ts:458`) | Group events | **Reject.** The mode gate exists because shared lines genuinely *cannot* subscribe to `groups.subscribeEvents()`. `message.read` arrives on the `messages` stream that both shared and dedicated lines already consume — there is no capability difference to derive from. Deriving anyway would mean "dedicated lines silently get a new message type," which is arbitrary *and* still a behavior break for every dedicated-line consumer. |
| **`configSchema` boolean** | Would be the first in this repo (`packages/slack/src/types.ts:60` `isFromMe` is a message field, not config) | **RECOMMEND.** Explicit, type-checked at the call site, visible in code review, works identically in shared/dedicated/cloud/explicit-clients modes, testable in-process with no cloud stub, zero round-trip. Cost: one optional field on a `strictObject` — fully additive, and Zod v4 omits absent optionals so `parse({}) → {}` stays true. |
| **Project-level cloud flag** (`projectConfig.profile.imessageReadReceipts`, mirroring `imessageSynced` at `stream.ts:453`) | Contact-card auto-share | **Reject.** `ProjectProfile` is `[key: string]: unknown` (`packages/core/src/utils/cloud.ts`) so it is untypeable and invisible in the app's source. It requires `projectId`/`projectSecret`, so it silently cannot gate explicit-client setups (`index.ts:483-499`). Most importantly it is the wrong *kind* of switch: `imessageSynced` toggles an ops-side side effect, whereas this changes the shape of the data the app's own loop receives. Ops must not be able to remotely inject a new message type into a deployed app. |

**Recommendation: `imessage.config({ readReceipts: true })`, default off.** Exact diff in §3.10.

**Why default-off is non-negotiable here.** `spectrum-ts` is at 12.4.0 with a widely-deployed consumer API of `for await (…) { switch (message.content.type) { … } }`. Many apps have a `default:` arm that replies to the user, or an `else` that pipes the message into an LLM. Turning read receipts on by default means: every message the agent sends in a 10-person group generates up to 10 new inbound Messages, each of which those apps would answer. Worst case is worse still — because `chats.markRead` marks *every* unread message in a chat, a peer opening a chat with 5 unread agent messages plausibly emits 5 events × 10 participants = 50 inbound Messages from one chat-open. That is a denial-of-service against a naive consumer loop, delivered by an SDK minor version.

**Naming.** `readReceipts` is my pick — it reads well at the call site and there is no competing meaning inside `imessage.config({…})` (the outbound half is never configured, it's `space.read()`). If you want zero ambiguity at the cost of terseness, `inboundReadReceipts` is the safe alternative; decide once, because renaming later is a breaking change.

---

## 5. Engineering concerns

### 5.1 Ordering, dedup, and `deliveredSinceCursor`

`id: ${messageGuid}:read:${sequence}` is derived purely from the event, so the *same* event replayed through catch-up produces the *same* id it produced live — which is exactly what `deliveredSinceCursor.has(item.id)` (`resumable-stream.ts:253`) needs to suppress the duplicate. `sequence` is monotonic per line, so N participants reading the same message get N distinct ids, and the `read` namespace cannot collide with `${messageGuid}:reaction:${sequence}`.

Changing away from the current catch-all id (`message.read:${messageGuid}:${sequence}`) is safe: `item.id` is never persisted — it lives only in the in-memory `Set` at `resumable-stream.ts:202`, which is cleared on cursor advance (`:243-245`), compacted after a catch-up flush (`:480-486`), and discarded on cursor rejection (`:322-323`). Only the *cursor* (`String(event.sequence)`) survives a reconnect, and that is untouched.

Two behaviors worth noting in review: suppressed receipts (flag off, or self-actor) still call `deliveredSinceCursor.add(item.id)` at `:260` — harmless, bounded by the same clear/compact logic. And `advanceCursor` regression-guards on the numeric cursor (`:158-169`), which read events satisfy like every other message event.

### 5.2 Cache misses — failure mode, and whether to guard

**Failure mode of a miss:** one extra unary `client.messages.get` + `rebuildFromAppleMessage` **on the critical path of the per-line message stream**. Because `resumable-stream.ts:384-385` awaits `processLive` serially inside `for await`, that RPC head-of-line-blocks every real inbound message on that line for its duration.

**But the amplification is much smaller than it first looks.** `resolveTargetMessage` calls `cacheMessage` on success, so in a 10-person group, participant #1's receipt pays one RPC and *warms the cache*; participants #2-#10 are free. Amplification is O(distinct messages read), not O(messages × participants). Combined with `cacheRemoteOutbound` (`index.ts:112-131`) caching every send at send time, the steady-state hit rate should be near 100%.

**The real gap is cold start.** Per finding #6 above, `stream.ts:121-123` early-returns self-sent `message.received` events *without* caching, so the cache is populated with our outbound messages only by `cacheRemoteOutbound`. After a restart, catch-up will replay read receipts for pre-restart sends and every one of them misses.

**Recommendation for v1: cache-first, one RPC on miss, drop on failure — identical to reactions.** Rationale: cache-only would mean "read receipts silently stop working after every deploy," which is a bad first impression for a public SDK feature; the self-warming property bounds the group amplification; and the opt-in flag bounds the blast radius to apps that asked for it. Document the cold-start burst in the provider docs.

**Rejected alternatives, worth recording:**
- *Cache self-sent messages at `stream.ts:121-123`.* Would close the restart gap, but costs a full `rebuildFromAppleMessage` (including attachment metadata and possibly a reply-target RPC via `resolveReplyTarget`) for every self-sent message, on every line, on every catch-up — a much larger tax than the one it avoids.
- *Cache-only, no RPC.* Cheapest and fully non-blocking, but loses all post-restart receipts.

**Optional hardening (Phase 5, not required for v1):** a per-client bounded `Set<string>` of guids that failed to resolve, keyed by client through a `WeakMap` exactly like `getMessageCache` (`packages/imessage/src/cache.ts:85-100`). Without it, a message that has been purged or unsent re-pays a failing RPC for each of the group's N participants. ~15 lines, self-contained in `read-receipts.ts`, no change to reaction behavior. Ship it only if telemetry shows failed resolutions are non-trivial.

One pre-existing wart to note, not to fix here: `resolveTargetMessage`'s bare `catch { return; }` swallows errors marked `retryable`, so they never reach `skipUnmappable`'s rethrow (`stream.ts:89-91`) and the event is dropped rather than retried via catch-up. That is exactly today's reaction behavior; changing it would change reaction semantics and belongs in its own PR.

### 5.3 `timestamp`: `readAt`, not `occurredAt`

Use **`event.readAt`**.

The principle already encoded in this codebase is "`Message.timestamp` = when the action happened." For reactions and group events, `occurredAt` *is* that instant — those event arms carry no more specific field. `message.read` does: `readAt` is when the reader's device marked it read, propagated from the peer; `occurredAt` is when the bridge observed the event. Those can differ materially when a receipt arrives via Continuity sync after a delay, and in that case `occurredAt` would report a read that happened minutes ago as happening now. Since the entire value of a read receipt is *when*, using the less-precise field defeats the feature.

The `?? event.occurredAt` fallback guards a proto-default-empty timestamp decoding to `undefined` despite the non-optional TS type — the same defensive shape as `message.dateCreated ?? new Date()` at `inbound.ts:437`.

**Do not add a `readAt` provider extra.** `messageSchema` (`packages/imessage/src/types.ts:70-74`) stays untouched: with `timestamp === readAt`, a `readAt` extra would be pure duplication, and every field added to `messageSchema` is permanent public API. If someone later needs the observation time, `occurredAt` can be added as an extra additively.

### 5.4 Group chats — yes, N receipts per message, and that is correct

One `message.read` event per participant, so one inbound `read` Message per participant, all sharing `content.target.id` and differing in `sender` and `timestamp`.

This is the only shape that answers the question the feature exists to answer — *who* has read it. Coalescing into one event carrying a reader list would require buffering plus an arbitrary debounce window, would have to guess when the set is complete, and would destroy the per-reader timestamp. The stream contract (one event in, zero-or-one messages out, cursor advances) also has no place to hold that state. Consumers who want "everyone has read it" aggregate by `content.target.id` against `space.getMembers()` — see the snippet in §1. This must be called out explicitly in the docs; it is the number-one thing that will surprise people.

Worst-case volume, for the docs: a peer opening a chat marks *every* unread message read, so one chat-open can emit (unread agent messages) × (participants) events.

### 5.5 Absent `actor` — drop, like reactions

Drop. `group-events.ts:129-135` states the discriminant precisely: "Unlike reactions — where an event without an actor is dropped because the actor is itself the substance — membership/rename/avatar changes surface even when the platform recorded no actor: the state change is the payload."

Read receipts are unambiguously in the reactions camp. `content.target` is a message we already have; `timestamp` alone is not actionable. Worse, a senderless receipt actively corrupts the primary use case: a consumer tallying "3 of 5 have read this" would count anonymous receipts as distinct readers or as duplicates depending on how they key. Emitting `sender: undefined` here would be emitting noise that looks like signal.

Consequence for consumers, and this is a nice guarantee to document: **`message.sender` is always defined on an inbound iMessage read receipt.** (It is still typed `User | undefined` because the framework-level `Message.sender` is.)

Deliberate non-goal for v1: for a **DM** a senderless receipt is technically unambiguous, and the peer could be synthesized from the chat guid (`any;-;+15550111`). That is a plausible follow-up, but it would make `sender` present-in-DMs / absent-in-groups, which is a worse contract than "always present."

### 5.6 Backward compatibility and semver

**Nothing breaks.**

- **Default behavior is byte-identical.** With `readReceipts` unset, `toMessageItem`'s read branch returns the same `{ cursor, id, values: [] }` skip item the catch-all returned; only `item.id`'s string changes, and that string is in-memory-only (§5.1).
- **No type-level change to the public surface.** `Content`, `Read`, and `Message` are untouched — `readSchema` has been in the `contentSchema` union since it shipped (`content/types.ts:77`). The only `.d.ts` delta is a new optional boolean on the iMessage config. Existing exhaustive `switch (message.content.type)` statements already had to handle `"read"` to typecheck, so no consumer's compile breaks.
- **`wrapNestedContent`'s new `read` arm is dead code until this ships** — it only fires when `content.type === "read"` *and* the target is a raw provider record. No provider emits that today, and the outbound path always carries a built `Message` (rejected by `isRawProviderRecord` at `build.ts:598-611`). Zero risk.
- **The webhook `read` case changes a degradation, not a behavior.** Inbound `read` over the native webhook currently falls through `mapContent`'s `default:` (`deserialize.ts:171-172`) to `{ type: "custom" }`. Nothing produces that today, so nothing observes the change.
- **Additive core export** (`readSchema` from `authoring.ts`).
- **Internal signature changes** (`toMessageItem`, `messageStream`, `clientStream`, `resolveReactionTarget`) are all module-private. The only exported-signature changes — `remote/stream.ts#messages` and `remote/api.ts#messages` — gain a *trailing optional* parameter. Both are reachable from `@spectrum-ts/imessage` only via deep import, and both stay call-compatible.

**Ship as `12.5.0`** (minor). The repo versions all packages in lockstep (every `packages/*/package.json` is `12.4.0`) and releases via `chore: release vX.Y.Z` commits (`973a8ac`) with `scripts/release/bump-version.ts`. A new opt-in feature plus additive core exports is textbook minor.

### 5.7 Other providers — out of scope, but the design pays for itself

**WhatsApp Business inbound read receipts are explicitly out of scope for this issue.** I verified `packages/whatsapp-business/src/` has **no** `statuses[]` handling at all today — only outbound `content.type === "read"` at `messages.ts:871`. Wiring `statuses[].status === "read"` would mean parsing a webhook branch the provider currently ignores entirely, mapping `statuses[].recipient_id` to a sender and `statuses[].id` to a target it has no content for. That is its own issue.

What this plan deliberately buys for it, at near-zero marginal cost:

- **No API decision is left open.** Because we reuse the `read` content type bidirectionally with `direction: "inbound"` + `content.target` + `sender`, WhatsApp lights up later with zero consumer-facing API change — a dev's `case "read":` arm works unchanged across providers.
- **The stub-target path already exists.** WhatsApp's status webhook gives you a message id and nothing else — exactly the shape `buildTargetRecord` (`deserialize.ts:295-309`) produces and the shape the new `wrapNestedContent` read arm wraps as outbound. That is the strongest reason to land §3.3 now even though iMessage does not use it.
- **The opt-in question will recur**, and WhatsApp should reuse the same `readReceipts` config-flag pattern for consistency.

Also out of scope, and to be stated in the issue so it does not get re-litigated:
- **`chat.markedRead`** on the `chats` stream: documented in `chat_types.proto:62` as "Chat unread count transitioned to zero (locally or via continuity)" — that is *our* unread count, an echo of our own `markRead`, not a peer receipt. We do not subscribe `chats.subscribeEvents()`.
- **`@spectrum-ts/imessage-local`**: no event stream to read from; its `configSchema` (`packages/imessage-local/src/types.ts:4`) is untouched, and outbound `read` already throws `UnsupportedError` there (`packages/imessage-local/src/index.ts:121-124`).
- **Telegram / Slack**: neither surfaces read state for bot conversations (already documented at `packages/core/src/content/read.ts:22-26`).

---

## 6. Testing plan

### `packages/imessage/test/remote/read-receipts.test.ts` — NEW
Mirrors `test/remote/group-events.test.ts` structurally and `test/remote/reactions.test.ts` for the event factory + `sdkMessage` + cache assertions. Add a `readEvent(overrides)` factory alongside `sdkMessage` (copied from `reactions.test.ts:52-66`, whose default is `isFromMe: true`, i.e. our own message — exactly the target we want).

1. `emits a read receipt with the reader as sender and our message as target` — cache-hit path via `MessageCache` pre-seeded with an outbound `IMessageMessage`. Asserts `content.type === "read"`, `content.target.id`, `content.target.direction === "outbound"`, `sender.id === actor.address`, `space === { id, type: "dm", phone }`, `id === "msg-guid:read:1"`.
2. `carries the actor's address, country, and service onto the reader` — mirrors `reactions.test.ts:166-191`; asserts the full `{ id, address, country, service }` ref.
3. `uses readAt, not occurredAt, for the timestamp` — event with `readAt` and `occurredAt` set to *different* dates; asserts `timestamp` equals `readAt`. **The test that pins §5.3.**
4. `falls back to occurredAt when readAt is absent` — `readAt: undefined` cast through the factory.
5. `drops an event with no actor` — `toEqual([])`, and asserts `messages.get` was never called (the actor guard must short-circuit *before* the RPC).
6. `drops a receipt whose target is one of their inbound messages` — `messages.get` resolves `sdkMessage({ isFromMe: false })

