# Inbound read receipts — iMessage (ENG-2101)

## Context

When a recipient reads a message the agent sent, `@photon-ai/advanced-imessage` delivers a `message.read` event on the stream we already subscribe to — and we drop it on the floor. `toMessageItem` (`packages/imessage/src/remote/stream.ts:113-161`) converts only `message.received` and `message.reactionAdded`; everything else hits a terminal `return { cursor, id, values: [] }`. The cursor advances, nothing is emitted.

Today `read` is outbound-only: `space.send(read(msg))` marks *their* messages read. This adds the inverse — **the other side read the message we sent** — as an inbound `Message` on `app.messages`.

Outcome: a dev can tell when, and by whom, each of their outbound messages was read, with per-reader attribution in group chats.

Out of scope, deliberately: `chat.markedRead` on the `chats` stream is documented in `chat_types.proto:62` as "Chat unread count transitioned to zero (locally or via continuity)" — that's *our own* unread count, an echo of our own `markRead`, not a peer receipt. We do not subscribe `chats.subscribeEvents()`. WhatsApp `statuses[].status === "read"` is a separate issue (the provider ignores the `statuses` webhook branch entirely today).

---

## What a consumer dev writes

```ts
for await (const [space, message] of app.messages) {
  switch (message.content.type) {
    case "text":
      await message.reply(`echo: ${message.content.text}`);
      break;

    case "read": {
      // Someone read a message we sent. `read` is one content type used in
      // both directions — same house rule as `reaction` and the membership
      // types. `direction` is the guard.
      if (message.direction !== "inbound") break;

      const target = message.content.target; // the message WE sent
      console.log(
        `${message.sender?.id} read "${target.id}" at ${message.timestamp.toISOString()}`
      );
      break;
    }
  }
}
```

The exact shapes:

```ts
message.direction            // "inbound"
message.sender               // User | undefined — the READER. Always set (§ Absent actor)
message.timestamp            // Date — event.readAt: when the reader's device marked it read
message.space                // Space — same object as the tuple's `space`

message.content              // narrowed to Read by `case "read":`
// export interface Read { type: "read"; target: Message }

message.content.target            // Message — fully built, not a stub
message.content.target.id         // the guid of the message the agent sent
message.content.target.direction  // "outbound"
message.content.target.content    // the text/attachment/etc. we sent
message.content.target.edit(…)    // available — target is built with outbound affordances
message.content.target.unsend()
```

iMessage-narrowed reader identity:

```ts
const im = imessage(message);
im.sender?.address;  // "+15551234567"
im.sender?.service;  // "iMessage" | "SMS" | "RCS" | "unknown"
```

Group chats emit **one message per reader**, sharing `content.target.id`. Aggregating "everyone has read it":

```ts
const readers = new Map<string, Set<string>>(); // target id -> reader ids

case "read": {
  if (message.direction !== "inbound") break;
  const set = readers.get(message.content.target.id) ?? new Set();
  set.add(message.sender?.id ?? "");
  readers.set(message.content.target.id, set);

  const members = await space.getMembers();
  if (set.size >= members.length - 1) {
    console.log(`everyone read ${message.content.target.id}`);
  }
  break;
}
```

**Invariant to document loudly:** `message.sender` is the reader; `message.content.target` is ours. Never the other way round.

---

## Design decisions

**1. Reuse the `read` content type bidirectionally.** No new content arm, no dotted taxonomy. This is the stated house rule (`packages/core/src/content/types.ts:28-33`: "Content values describe state changes and are direction-agnostic… The Message envelope carries `direction` and the actor") and the precedent set by ENG-1878 for inbound group events. `readSchema` is already in the `contentSchema` union (`types.ts:77`) — no union change needed, and existing exhaustive `switch` statements already compile.

The `read()` *builder*'s `direction !== "inbound"` throw (`content/read.ts:53-57`) is an outbound-API guard and **stays**. Inbound receipts never go through the builder — providers call `readSchema.parse` directly, the same way `reactions.ts:52` does with `reactionSchema`.

**2. On by default, no config key.** Per your call. Consequence to accept knowingly: existing `for await` loops start seeing a content type they've never received, and volume is one event per participant per message read — a peer opening a chat with 5 unread agent messages in a 10-person group can emit up to 50 messages. The two filters below are what bound it; the docs must call it out.

**3. Never trust `event.isFromMe`.** `MessageChangeEvent.is_from_me` has no proto comment and most plausibly describes the *underlying message* — which for a genuine receipt is ours, i.e. `true`. Passing a read event to the existing `isEventFromCurrentAccount` (`stream.ts:56-63`) would therefore suppress 100% of useful receipts. Self-suppression instead comes from two checks that don't depend on it:

- cheap pre-filter: `actor.address === phone` (a dedicated line reading in its own Messages app);
- load-bearing: **the resolved target must be `direction: "outbound"`**. A peer's receipt always points at a message we sent. That single check also suppresses the echo of our own `chats.markRead()`, which marks *their* inbound messages and resolves `direction: "inbound"`.

**4. Reuse, don't duplicate.** Extract the cache→fetch→rebuild resolver currently private in `reactions.ts:58-85` instead of copy-pasting it.

