## Engineering notes

**Ordering and dedup.** `id: ${messageGuid}:read:${sequence}` is derived purely from the event, so catch-up replay produces the id it produced live — which is what `deliveredSinceCursor.has(item.id)` (`packages/core/src/utils/resumable-stream.ts:253`) needs. `sequence` is monotonic per line, so N readers get N distinct ids, and the `read` namespace can't collide with `${guid}:reaction:${sequence}`. Changing away from the current catch-all id is safe: `item.id` is never persisted — it lives only in the in-memory `Set` at `:202`, cleared on cursor advance. Only the cursor (`String(event.sequence)`) survives a reconnect, and that's untouched.

**Cache misses cost one RPC, on the critical path.** `resumable-stream.ts:384-385` awaits `processLive` serially, so the fetch head-of-line-blocks real inbound messages on that line. Amplification is smaller than it looks: `resolveTargetMessage` calls `cacheMessage` on success, so in a 10-person group reader #1 pays one RPC and warms the cache; #2–#10 are free. Amplification is O(distinct messages read), not O(messages × readers). Combined with `cacheRemoteOutbound` (`index.ts:112-131`) caching every send, steady-state hit rate is near 100%.

The gap is cold start: `stream.ts:121-123` early-returns self-sent `message.received` events *before* caching, so our outbound messages enter the cache only via `cacheRemoteOutbound`. After a restart, catch-up replays receipts for pre-restart sends and every one misses; those that can't be fetched are dropped. Accepted for v1 — cache-only would mean "receipts silently stop working after every deploy." Optional Phase-5 hardening if telemetry warrants: a bounded `Set<string>` of guids that failed to resolve, keyed by client through a `WeakMap` like `getMessageCache` (`cache.ts:85-100`), so a purged message doesn't re-pay a failing RPC per reader.

Pre-existing wart, not fixed here: the bare `catch { return; }` swallows `retryable` errors so they never reach `skipUnmappable`'s rethrow (`stream.ts:89-91`). That's today's reaction behavior; changing it belongs in its own PR.

**Timestamp is `readAt`.** `occurredAt` is when the bridge observed the event; these diverge materially when a receipt arrives via Continuity sync, and using the less-precise field defeats the whole feature.

**Groups emit N messages, one per reader.** That's the only shape that answers who read it. Coalescing would need buffering plus an arbitrary debounce, would have to guess when the reader set is complete, and would destroy per-reader timestamps. Consumers aggregate by `content.target.id` — see the snippet above. This is the number-one thing that will surprise people; it must be prominent in the docs.

**Absent actor → drop**, matching reactions. `group-events.ts:129-135` states the discriminant: an actor-less membership change still carries meaning because the state change is the payload; for a receipt the reader *is* the payload. Emitting `sender: undefined` would be noise that looks like signal. Consequence worth documenting as a guarantee: **`sender` is always defined on an inbound iMessage read receipt** (still typed `User | undefined` because the framework-level `Message.sender` is).

**Compatibility.** Nothing breaks at the type level: `readSchema` has been in the `Content` union since it shipped, so exhaustive `switch (message.content.type)` already compiles. What changes at runtime is that `app.messages` starts yielding a content type existing loops have never seen — a deployed app whose `default:` arm replies to the user will now reply to read receipts. Ship as **12.5.0** with that stated prominently at the top of the release notes; if you'd rather treat any new inbound surface as breaking, 13.0.0 is the conservative call. Everything else is additive: one core export, one dead-until-Cloud webhook case, one `wrapNestedContent` arm that is inert on the outbound path.

